Ulam's prime spiral in 3D: the numbers 1, 2, 3, ... on a square spiral
of unit cells in the xy plane (1 in the middle, 2 to its right, then
counter-clockwise), one shape per number. The primes line up on
diagonals, as Stanislaw Ulam saw in 1963.

Calculated by mb3d-rs itself (the code above is only a placeholder; the
original Mandelbulb3D shows nothing with this formula).

Mode 0: a plate per number, a tower on each prime.
Mode 1: a column per number, higher the more divisors it has (the
primes are the low streets).
Mode 2: a sphere per number, larger the more divisors it has.
Mode 3: the numbers on cube shells around the middle (each shell's
bottom face as the square spiral, then the ring of each layer, then the
top face), a cube on each prime.
Rings: the size of the spiral (60 rings: the numbers up to 14641; mode 3
at most 120).
Height: the height of the towers (mode 0) or of the columns (mode 1,
growing with the logarithm of the divisor count).
Gap: the space between the shapes, as a part of a cell.
Colour scale: the colour value (orbit trap, "2nd choice" in the
Lighting) is the logarithm of the divisor count; this scales it.
